steps.body:
An exchange won't pause while you rebuild a lopsided tree of live orders, so the tree has to change shape in place, a little at a time, and never break its sorted order. A **rotation** does that: it lifts a child above its parent and keeps every key in sorted order. A left rotation at `x` lifts its right child `y`:

```text
    x                      y
   / \                    / \
  A   y        ->        x   C
     / \                / \
    B   C              A   B
```

`A`, `B`, and `C` are whole subtrees, and any of them can be empty. Read either picture left to right and you get A, x, B, y, C, so the BST rule still holds everywhere. Only `B` changes parent. Its keys lie between x and y, so it moves from `y`'s left to `x`'s right. Three pointers change, so a rotation costs O(1) however big the subtrees are. Run it on the chain 1, 2, 3:

```cpp harness
struct TreeNode {
    int value;
    TreeNode* left;
    TreeNode* right;
};
{{code}}
TreeNode* three = new TreeNode{3, nullptr, nullptr};
TreeNode* two = new TreeNode{2, nullptr, three};
TreeNode* root = new TreeNode{1, nullptr, two};
root = rotateLeft(root);
std::cout << root->value << " with children " << root->left->value << " and " << root->right->value << '\n';
delete root->left;
delete root->right;
delete root;
```

```cpp
TreeNode* rotateLeft(TreeNode* x) {
    TreeNode* y = x->right;
    x->right = y->left;   // B moves across
    y->left = x;
    return y;             // the new top of this subtree
}
```

Three levels become two, with 2 on top and 1 and 3 below it. A right rotation is the mirror image: it lifts the left child, with `left` and `right` swapped everywhere.

The subtree has a new top, so whatever pointed at `x` must now point at `y`. Follow the rule `insert` follows in *Binary Trees and Binary Search Trees*: return the new top and let the caller store it, `root = rotateLeft(root);` at the root or `node->right = rotateLeft(node->right);` lower down. Drop the returned pointer and the parent still points at `x`, which now sits below `y`, so `y` and `C` fall out of the tree. Balanced trees are built from this one move: after each insert they check a rule, heights for AVL and colors for red-black, and rotate where one side has grown too tall.

