steps.body:
An index of order ids has to stay short after every insert, whatever order the ids arrive in, since no one can stop the feed to rebalance it. An **AVL tree**, named for its inventors Adelson-Velsky and Landis, does that with one rule at every node: the heights of its two subtrees differ by at most 1. Each node stores its subtree's height, so checking the rule is one subtraction. The **balance** is the left height minus the right height, and it must stay -1, 0, or 1.

An insert walks down like a plain BST insert, so only nodes on that path can change height. The recursion returns up the same path, and at each node `rebalance` recomputes the height and rotates when the balance has reached 2 or -2. The fix depends on which way the tall side leans:

- Left-left: the left child is tall on its own left. One right rotation at the node fixes it.
- Right-right: the mirror, one left rotation.
- Left-right: the left child is tall on its right, a bend. A right rotation alone would only bend it the other way, so first rotate left at the child to straighten it, then right at the node.
- Right-left: the mirror, a right rotation at the child, then a left one at the node.

```text
left-right at 30:    30           30          20
                    /            /           /  \
                  10     ->    20     ->   10    30
                    \          /
                     20      10
```

Rotations move nodes up and down, so they recompute heights, the lower node first, since the upper one's height depends on it:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

struct AvlNode {
    int value;
    int height;
    AvlNode* left;
    AvlNode* right;
};

int height(const AvlNode* node) { return node == nullptr ? 0 : node->height; }

AvlNode* fixHeight(AvlNode* node) {
    node->height = 1 + std::max(height(node->left), height(node->right));
    return node;
}

{{code}}

int main() {
    AvlNode* ten = new AvlNode{10, 1, nullptr, nullptr};
    AvlNode* twenty = new AvlNode{20, 2, ten, nullptr};
    AvlNode* root = new AvlNode{30, 3, twenty, nullptr};
    root = rotateRight(root);
    std::cout << "root " << root->value << ", height " << root->height << '\n';
    delete root->left;
    delete root->right;
    delete root;
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
AvlNode* rotateRight(AvlNode* y) {
    AvlNode* x = y->left;
    y->left = x->right;
    x->right = fixHeight(y);   // y is below x now
    return fixHeight(x);
}
```

On the left-left chain 30, 20, 10 it prints root 20 with height 2. After an insert's rotation the subtree is back to the height it had before the insert, so nothing above it needs another one, and an insert makes at most two rotations. The rule keeps the height under about 1.44 log2(n) in the worst case.

